Z||000000||LAMBO||08-09-2001||03:52 PM||||Back in January, you had a problem with your 88 V6 starting hard. Slow to catch and fire (clear the props) I think is what your roomate said. Anyway, what ended up being the fix. I have an 88 doing the same exact thing. Runs great, just slow to catch and fire on the first crank. <BR>Todd||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||08-09-2001||04:08 PM||||I <I><B>still</I></B> don't know...havnt really gotten around to sourcing the problem yet. I have decided, however, that Im not going to...Im just gonna dump a sbc 350 in its place!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>(However, if I had to take a wild guess, I'd say it is the cold start injector.) Good luck! <P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||LAMBO||08-09-2001||04:37 PM||||Well, thats one way to fix the problem. Thanks for responding so quick. I did read in another archive post that when a fuel pump relay fails, or is starting to fail, the oil pressure relay takes over and it doesn't kick in until there's at least 5 lbs. of pressure, hence slow to start on first crank. For $10 bucks and something easy to get at, I might try a new relay first. If only I could be so lucky.<BR>Later, Todd||||reg||6
